## Linear Assignment Problems
- The setup of the most basic variant of linear assignment problems
  - There $n$ agents and $n$ tasks
  - We have a cost matrix $C$ where $C_{ij}$ is the cost of assigning agent $i$ to task $j$
  - Goal: assign each agent to a task while minimizing the total cost
  - Enforce 1-to-1 assignment
  
- Parallels between LAP and cross-modality single cell integration
  - $n$ cells from RNA-seq data (agents) and $n$ cells from ATAC-seq data (tasks)
  - $C_{ij}$ describes the similarity between cell $i$ from RNA-seq and cell $j$ from ATAC-seq
      - We are free to choose a suitable way to calculate the value of $C_{ij}$
  - Goal: assign each cell from RNA-seq to a cell from ATAC-seq while maximizing the total similarity, i.e. minimize total dissimilarity
  - Enforce 1-to-1 assignment

---

## Linear Assignment Problems

$$\begin{array}{ll}
\mathbf{C}=\begin{pmatrix}
c_{11} & c_{12} & \dots & c_{1n} \\
c_{21} & c_{22} & \dots & c_{2n} \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
c_{n1} & c_{n2} & \dots & c_{nn}
\end{pmatrix}
&
\mathbf{X}=\begin{pmatrix}
x_{11} & x_{12} & \dots & x_{1n} \\
x_{21} & x_{22} & \dots & x_{2n} \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
x_{n1} & x_{n2} & \dots & x_{nn}
\end{pmatrix}
\end{array}$$

$$\text{minimize } \sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}c_{ij}x_{ij} \\
\text{subject to } \sum_{j=1}^{n}x_{ij}=1,\ \forall i=1,\dots,n \\
\text{and } x_{ij} \in \{0, 1\},\ \forall i,j=1,\dots,n$$

- **This is the same setup as linear programming, but with a special requirement that x can only take values of 0 or 1.**

