## Hungarian Algorithm
<div style="text-align: center;">
![Hungarian Algorithm Description](images/Hungarian Algorithm Cropped.png)


<div style="position: absolute; bottom: 20px; width: 90%; font-size: 0.7em; color: gray;">
*Source: [Towards Data Science](https://towardsdatascience.com/optimum-assignment-and-the-hungarian-algorithm-8b1027628028/)*


???
1. Row Reduction: Subtract the minimum value in each row from all values in that row. (Ensures at least one zero per row).
2. Column Reduction: Subtract the minimum value in each column (of the new matrix) from all values in that column. (Ensures at least one zero per column).
3. Cover Zeros: Draw the minimum number of horizontal and/or vertical lines (k) required to cover all zeros in the matrix.
4. Check for Optimality:

    If k=n (the size of the matrix), an optimal assignment can be made using the current zeros. Go to Step 7.
    If k<n, the current zero set is not sufficient. Proceed to Step 5 to create more zeros.

5. Improve the Matrix: Find the smallest value (δ) among all uncovered elements.
6. Adjust Matrix:

    Subtract δ from all uncovered values.
    Add δ to all values at the intersection of two lines (doubly covered).
    Leave singly covered values unchanged.
    Return to Step 3 (Cover Zeros) with the new matrix.

7. Extract Assignment: Once k=n, find a perfect assignment (one zero per row and column) using only the zero cells in the final matrix. This assignment is an optimal solution with the minimum total cost.

---

## Hungarian Algorithm
- Intuition: **If you subtract a constant value from every element in a row or every element in a column of the cost matrix, the optimal assignment remains the same.**
  - All the operations preserve the relative cost structure of the matrix while helping us find the cheapest assignment.
- Subtract the row or column minimum: "standardizing" the cost of each agent or each task
  - Some agent or some task might be inherently costlier than other agents or tasks
  - By finding the 0's, we find the relatively least costly assignment for each agent and task
- Subtract the minimum uncovered value ($\delta$) from all uncovered cells:
  - If $c_{ij}$ is uncovered, it means that agent $i$ and task $j$ are both unassigned
  - Subtracting from all uncovered cells allows at least one more agent and one more task to be assigned
- Cells covered by two lines:
  - Adding $\delta$ to all doubly covered cells preserves the relative cost structure among all cells

???
Subtracting $\delta$ from all uncovered cells is equivalent to subtract $\delta$ from all uncovered **rows**and adding $\delta$ to all covered **columns**. With this operation, doubly covered cells have $\delta$ added to them.

