## Junker-Volgenant Algorithm
- Much faster than the Hungarian algorithm, especially for large matrices
- Solves a  dual problem of the Hungarian algorithm 
- Hungarian algorithm implicitly uses "potentials" (i.e. excess cost of a cell) while JV explicitly uses this
- Iteratively assign tasks to each agent using shortest-path algorithms to avoid having to find the optimal matching at each step, which can be computationally intensive
- Worse-case computation complexity:
  - JV: $O(n^3)$
  - Hungarian: $O(n^4)$

---

## Auction Algorithm
- Analogous to an actual auction where agents bid for their most-valued tasks
- Quantities
  - $c_{ij}$ : how much agent $i$ values task $j$
  - $p_{j}$ : the price of task $j$
  - $d_{ij} = c_{ij} - p_{j}$ : the surplus of agent $i$ for being assigned to task $j$
- Goal: maximize the total surplus of all agents
- The speed of the algorithm can be tuned by an $\epsilon$ parameter, which is the increments with which agents bid, i.e. $\epsilon = p_{j}^{(b)} - p_{j}^{(b-1)}$

